WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.960
So have you ever hugged a close friend after a really long, exhausting week?

00:07.100 --> 00:08.540
Oh, absolutely. We all have.

00:08.660 --> 00:12.080
Right. And you know that exact moment the embrace happens, right?

00:12.120 --> 00:17.460
Like you feel that immediate physical wave of relief just sort of wash over you.

00:17.460 --> 00:25.400
The tension physically leaves your shoulders, your jaw unclenches, and your breathing slows down.

00:25.540 --> 00:29.160
Your heart rate actually seems to settle into a much slower rhythm.

00:29.160 --> 00:31.640
Yeah, it's a very real, very physical sensation.

00:31.940 --> 00:37.080
It is. And if you're anything like most people who, you know, read health blogs or pop psychology,

00:37.080 --> 00:41.900
you've probably thought to yourself in that moment, oh, I really needed that oxytocin hit.

00:41.980 --> 00:43.580
Right. The famous oxytocin hit.

00:43.660 --> 00:48.840
Exactly. I mean, society has basically trained us to immediately credit this tiny little molecule.

00:49.000 --> 00:52.660
I think it's a sequence of just nine amino acids for all of that magic.

00:52.920 --> 00:56.520
We're taught that we walk around with these like internal reservoirs of affection.

00:56.520 --> 01:01.880
And when they run dry, we crave human contact just to refill the chemical tanks.

01:01.900 --> 01:03.680
It's a very mechanical way of looking at it.

01:03.920 --> 01:04.220
Totally.

01:04.980 --> 01:09.460
Well, today we are going to completely shatter that idea.

01:09.580 --> 01:12.560
We're going to dismantle the whole love hormone myth.

01:12.720 --> 01:17.020
But I promise you, we are going to do it in a way that actually makes your relationships,

01:17.380 --> 01:23.260
your affections, and your biological drive for connection feel infinitely more profound, not less.

01:23.260 --> 01:27.780
I love that because the myth we're deconstructing today is incredibly pervasive.

01:27.980 --> 01:34.320
I mean, you can hardly read an article about relationships or parenting or stress management

01:34.320 --> 01:39.220
without tripping over the word oxytocin presented as this like ultimate goal of human interaction.

01:39.300 --> 01:39.980
So it's everywhere.

01:40.280 --> 01:46.080
Right. But what replaces this simplistic biological story is a reality that is just staggering in its complexity.

01:46.260 --> 01:48.100
Yeah. And that's exactly what we're getting into.

01:48.100 --> 01:53.360
For today's deep dive, we are looking at a truly fascinating paper published in August 2026.

01:53.560 --> 01:57.840
It's by an independent researcher named Fliction, and it's titled Rain on the Parade.

01:58.120 --> 02:01.560
Why Wanting Affection is Not the Same as Craving Oxytocin.

02:01.620 --> 02:02.380
It's such a great title.

02:02.760 --> 02:03.420
It really is.

02:03.800 --> 02:10.140
And the mission of this deep dive is to basically rescue the sheer beauty of human connection

02:10.140 --> 02:13.140
from a really persistent scientific misunderstanding.

02:13.140 --> 02:18.840
And to do that, we have to look closely at how a single neurobiological fact got twisted

02:18.840 --> 02:20.840
into this massive cultural phenomenon.

02:21.100 --> 02:22.840
So, OK, let's unpack this.

02:23.000 --> 02:23.420
Let's do it.

02:23.880 --> 02:27.220
We all know oxytocin as the love hormone, right?

02:27.280 --> 02:28.480
It's printed on greeting hearts.

02:28.560 --> 02:30.080
It's a buzzword in couples therapy.

02:30.380 --> 02:34.000
It's, you know, the punchline of jokes about needing a hug.

02:34.080 --> 02:34.620
Oh, for sure.

02:35.000 --> 02:39.740
But this source argues that our culture has committed a massive collective conceptual error.

02:39.740 --> 02:43.640
The core of that error is basically conflating a mechanism with a motive.

02:44.360 --> 02:48.620
So just because oxytocin participates in the process of attachment, that does not mean

02:48.620 --> 02:51.540
attachment is secretly just a desire to acquire a molecule.

02:51.840 --> 02:51.960
Yeah.

02:52.100 --> 02:56.460
And to set the stage for why this distinction actually matters so much, the author provides

02:56.460 --> 03:00.560
this framing analogy that I think clarifies the entire issue perfectly.

03:00.820 --> 03:01.780
OK, lay it on us.

03:01.960 --> 03:04.460
So imagine a beautiful blossoming garden.

03:04.460 --> 03:10.280
You have complex root systems, intricate soil ecology, vibrant flowers, and this whole

03:10.280 --> 03:12.040
network of biological activity.

03:13.280 --> 03:18.140
Now, we know as a fundamental fact that rain participates in the growth of that garden.

03:18.380 --> 03:18.500
Right.

03:18.740 --> 03:23.020
Rain is an absolute biological necessity for the ecosystem to even function.

03:23.320 --> 03:23.700
Exactly.

03:24.180 --> 03:28.100
But that requirement does not mean the garden is somehow trying to obtain rain.

03:28.360 --> 03:28.760
Huh.

03:29.440 --> 03:33.920
The garden doesn't possess a conscious drive to acquire water droplets.

03:33.920 --> 03:38.220
And the rain certainly doesn't fall on command just because the flowers are experiencing

03:38.220 --> 03:38.800
a deficit.

03:39.620 --> 03:44.200
And as we're going to see when we dig into the empirical data, neither does oxytocin.

03:44.280 --> 03:44.540
Wow.

03:44.740 --> 03:44.920
OK.

03:45.140 --> 03:50.020
So the garden is an ecosystem reacting to its environment, not some like machine hunting

03:50.020 --> 03:51.500
for a specific fuel source.

03:51.580 --> 03:51.920
Which I see.

03:51.960 --> 03:54.560
But that raises a really interesting psychological question, right?

03:54.980 --> 03:59.600
If the science never actually said we are walking around craving a molecule, why did we all

03:59.600 --> 04:01.840
buy into this idea so easily in the first place?

04:01.840 --> 04:06.840
Like, why are we so eager to describe our deepest emotional needs in such sterile chemical terms?

04:06.840 --> 04:11.320
Well, what's fascinating here is the psychological appeal of that specific narrative.

04:12.200 --> 04:17.680
The author points out that the oxytocin craving explanation has everything required to be a

04:17.680 --> 04:19.660
wildly successful piece of pop science.

04:20.080 --> 04:23.080
It offers this illusion of mastery.

04:23.260 --> 04:23.820
Oh, I see.

04:24.100 --> 04:26.480
Because human relationships are inherently messy.

04:26.480 --> 04:26.960
Exactly.

04:27.680 --> 04:33.560
They are fraught with history, miscommunication, subjective nuance, and a whole lot of emotional

04:33.560 --> 04:33.980
risk.

04:34.080 --> 04:36.120
And they're incredibly difficult to quantify.

04:36.340 --> 04:41.720
You can't put a number on grief or loneliness or the feeling of being understood by a friend.

04:41.860 --> 04:43.740
But you can put a number on a molecular concentration.

04:44.120 --> 04:44.400
Right.

04:44.800 --> 04:50.000
Pop science took something impossibly abstract and frustrating and basically replaced it with

04:50.000 --> 04:51.020
the tangible noun.

04:51.020 --> 04:57.080
It's so much easier to say my oxytocin is low than to say I feel profoundly disconnected from

04:57.080 --> 04:58.780
the people around me and I don't know why.

04:58.900 --> 05:01.340
It replaces a relational problem with a mechanical one.

05:01.420 --> 05:06.640
And the author traces the exact moment where the scientific truth was quietly substituted with

05:06.640 --> 05:08.100
this pop culture mythology.

05:08.260 --> 05:08.500
Okay.

05:08.540 --> 05:09.260
When did that happen?

05:09.260 --> 05:15.280
So, in the early days of neuroendocrinology, researchers proved a very specific proposition,

05:15.800 --> 05:19.280
which was simply, oxytocin participates in social behavior.

05:20.060 --> 05:25.500
Through studies on various mammals, particularly voles, they established that this neuropeptide

05:25.500 --> 05:30.580
was active in the neural pathways associated with bonding and maternal care and social memory.

05:30.720 --> 05:32.900
And that part is an undisputed fact, right?

05:33.020 --> 05:33.180
Right.

05:33.180 --> 05:38.460
Oxytocin is a real peptide hormone produced in the hypothalamus and its involvement in

05:38.460 --> 05:44.100
reproduction and lactation and social processing is a totally well-documented biological reality.

05:44.480 --> 05:47.820
Nobody's denying the existence or the basic function of the molecule itself.

05:48.060 --> 05:48.160
Right.

05:48.220 --> 05:49.140
The molecule is real.

05:49.620 --> 05:53.820
But popular culture took that fact, the fact that oxytocin participates in social behavior

05:53.820 --> 05:57.320
and perceptibly swapped it with a completely different claim.

05:57.700 --> 06:01.200
They swapped it to social behavior is undertaken to obtain oxytocin.

06:01.220 --> 06:01.760
Oh, wow.

06:02.000 --> 06:02.220
Yeah.

06:02.220 --> 06:06.820
The public narrative shifted the molecule from being a mechanism that helps facilitate

06:06.820 --> 06:10.040
an experience to being the ultimate goal of the experience.

06:10.180 --> 06:12.100
Wait, I need to push back on this logic for a second.

06:12.220 --> 06:12.560
Go for it.

06:12.680 --> 06:17.820
So, if I am in a laboratory and you give me an oxytocin nasal spray, which, you know,

06:17.920 --> 06:22.820
is a really common experimental setup in this field, and suddenly I act more trusting in

06:22.820 --> 06:28.700
an economic game where I gaze longer at faces on a screen, if you literally manipulate the

06:28.700 --> 06:33.280
chemical input and it reliably changes my social output, how can you argue that the chemical

06:33.280 --> 06:34.420
isn't the primary driver?

06:34.420 --> 06:37.460
I mean, I turn the dial on the molecule and the behavior changed.

06:37.840 --> 06:39.360
Doesn't that prove the molecule is the point?

06:39.700 --> 06:43.700
That is the exact logical leap that most pop science writers make.

06:43.900 --> 06:45.000
It seems completely intuitive.

06:45.480 --> 06:48.280
If A affects B, A must be the master controller.

06:48.280 --> 06:52.280
But the source material clarifies why that is a categorical mistake.

06:52.580 --> 06:52.780
How so?

06:53.040 --> 06:56.460
Just because manipulating a physiological mediator alters an outcome.

06:56.800 --> 06:59.940
That does not mean the mediator is the object the organism is seeking.

07:00.440 --> 07:05.180
A physiological process can be entirely necessary for producing an experience without being the

07:05.180 --> 07:07.540
thing the organism is actually attempting to obtain.

07:07.540 --> 07:10.080
Okay, let me make sure I'm translating this correctly for the listener.

07:10.480 --> 07:16.800
So when you miss someone, let's say a partner who's out of town, your biology is doing a ton

07:16.800 --> 07:22.260
of work to generate that feeling of longing, but the feeling is directed at them.

07:22.540 --> 07:23.500
Yes, exactly.

07:23.560 --> 07:27.980
They're not sitting on your couch thinking, oh man, my peptide levels are dropping to suboptimal

07:27.980 --> 07:28.740
concentrations.

07:29.060 --> 07:33.120
I require a mammalian body to restore my hypothalamic equilibrium.

07:33.480 --> 07:35.060
You just want your partner.

07:35.240 --> 07:36.060
You want the person.

07:36.060 --> 07:42.080
The neurochemistry is heavily involved in realizing your desire, but replacing the person with

07:42.080 --> 07:45.640
the chemistry doesn't actually deepen our understanding of the phenomenon.

07:46.040 --> 07:48.020
It merely changes the level of description.

07:48.400 --> 07:52.540
We move from describing a psychological reality to a biochemical one.

07:52.640 --> 07:56.880
Right, and then we mistakenly believe that changing the vocabulary is the exact same thing

07:56.880 --> 07:58.020
as explaining the motivation.

07:58.260 --> 08:02.860
So the translation is broken, but I mean, we still have to account for the physical reality

08:02.860 --> 08:08.880
of human emotion, because if oxytocin isn't what we are craving, why does social isolation

08:08.880 --> 08:10.900
feel so intensely physical?

08:11.260 --> 08:12.520
It really does, doesn't it?

08:12.760 --> 08:13.060
Yeah.

08:13.460 --> 08:17.000
When you are deeply lonely, it doesn't just feel like a passing thought.

08:17.320 --> 08:17.880
It hurts.

08:18.260 --> 08:22.120
It feels like an empty stomach or a parched throat or like you are physically cold.

08:22.400 --> 08:24.480
It mimics a biological deficit.

08:25.280 --> 08:29.280
Well, if we connect this to the bigger picture, we have to examine the concept of homeostatic

08:29.280 --> 08:29.760
drives.

08:29.960 --> 08:31.240
Okay, break that down for us.

08:31.240 --> 08:35.820
A homeostatic drive is a genuine physiological regulatory system.

08:36.340 --> 08:39.200
Think about hunger or thirst or your core body temperature.

08:39.900 --> 08:43.760
In a homeostatic system, a specific physiological condition becomes displaced.

08:44.500 --> 08:49.480
Your blood glucose drops or your body loses water or your internal temperature falls below

08:49.480 --> 08:50.860
98.6 degrees.

08:51.140 --> 08:56.120
And when that displacement happens, your nervous system triggers an alarm, motivating behavior

08:56.120 --> 08:58.000
to fix the specific deficit.

08:58.000 --> 09:03.420
Like the temperature drops, the biological thermostat kicks on, you shiver, you seek warmth, and

09:03.420 --> 09:04.640
the baseline is restored.

09:04.800 --> 09:06.460
It's a very clean feedback loop.

09:06.640 --> 09:09.720
It is an incredibly elegant evolutionary solution for survival.

09:10.200 --> 09:15.320
And because hunger and thirst operate through this clear homeostatic loop, it is remarkably

09:15.320 --> 09:20.740
tempting for us to treat every single neurochemical correlate of motivation as if it were a homeostat.

09:21.260 --> 09:21.960
Oh, I see.

09:22.540 --> 09:26.160
We feel the sharp physical pain of loneliness.

09:26.160 --> 09:29.000
We recognize that it feels similar to hunger.

09:29.640 --> 09:33.740
And we conclude, there must be an affection nutrient that I am depleted in.

09:33.880 --> 09:34.260
Exactly.

09:34.800 --> 09:38.720
And oxytocin was simply the most convenient molecule available to cast in that role.

09:38.840 --> 09:45.020
The paper actually uses some fantastic analogies to expose the absurdity of applying this homeostatic

09:45.020 --> 09:46.380
logic to everything we do.

09:46.840 --> 09:48.460
Let's talk about the sunset analogy.

09:48.540 --> 09:49.360
That's a great one.

09:49.360 --> 09:49.600
Yeah.

09:49.720 --> 09:53.820
So imagine you're standing on a beach watching a breathtakingly beautiful sunset.

09:54.640 --> 09:59.800
The photoreceptor cells in the retina of your eye are absolutely necessary for that experience.

10:00.120 --> 10:03.360
If your photoreceptors aren't firing, you aren't seeing the sunset.

10:03.640 --> 10:09.040
But you are not standing on the beach because you possess a psychological drive to seek photoreceptor

10:09.040 --> 10:09.560
activation.

10:09.720 --> 10:09.840
Right.

10:09.900 --> 10:12.560
The photoreceptors are the required mechanism of vision.

10:12.760 --> 10:14.820
They are not the motivation for looking at the sky.

10:15.220 --> 10:15.660
Exactly.

10:15.660 --> 10:16.800
Or consider walking.

10:16.960 --> 10:21.340
If you are walking home from work because you're eager to see your family, your motor

10:21.340 --> 10:22.580
neurons are firing rapidly.

10:22.980 --> 10:24.900
They are participating in every single step.

10:25.460 --> 10:29.140
But it would be ridiculous to say that your goal is to stimulate your motor neurons.

10:29.480 --> 10:29.740
Right.

10:29.960 --> 10:30.720
You just want to get home.

10:30.900 --> 10:31.120
Yeah.

10:31.240 --> 10:35.240
We easily recognize the difference between the mechanism and the goal when it comes to

10:35.240 --> 10:36.000
seeing or walking.

10:36.520 --> 10:41.400
But the moment we transition to emotions, we suddenly strip away the goal, the human connection,

10:41.400 --> 10:45.260
and claim the biological machinery is the only thing we ever actually wanted.

10:45.260 --> 10:49.180
We reduce the entire human experience to the maintenance of the machinery.

10:50.080 --> 10:54.020
But you know, the author is careful to point out that we cannot simply dismiss the fronical

10:54.020 --> 10:55.080
sensation of loneliness.

10:56.220 --> 10:59.020
Social motivation does have homeostatic-like features.

10:59.600 --> 11:01.320
Meaning the urgency is real.

11:01.540 --> 11:01.820
Yes.

11:02.300 --> 11:03.720
The urgency is very real.

11:04.240 --> 11:09.820
Research consistently shows that social isolation reliably activates stress pathways and social

11:09.820 --> 11:11.600
reunion reliably attenuates them.

11:11.600 --> 11:17.060
Mammals separated from their attachment figures exhibit profound, measurable changes in their

11:17.060 --> 11:17.620
physiology.

11:17.620 --> 11:23.540
But if they aren't suffering from an oxytocin deficit, what is actually causing that physical

11:23.540 --> 11:24.500
pain of separation?

11:24.640 --> 11:26.900
Like, what is regulating that felt urgency?

11:27.240 --> 11:32.260
The physiological systems most consistently implicated in regulating the painful feeling of

11:32.260 --> 11:38.480
missing someone are actually the stress axis, specifically the hypothalamic pituitary adrenal,

11:38.860 --> 11:43.600
or HPA axis, which manages cortisol, and the endogenous opioid system.

11:43.740 --> 11:45.180
Okay, we should break that down for the listener.

11:45.760 --> 11:50.460
The endogenous opioid system is our body's internal pain regulation network, right?

11:50.520 --> 11:55.740
It uses endorphins to manage physical pain, but it also heavily overlaps with social pain.

11:55.740 --> 12:01.220
When you're isolated, that opioid system signals distress in a way that is neurologically

12:01.220 --> 12:03.560
indistinguishable from actual physical injury.

12:03.760 --> 12:08.400
The regulatory pattern is absolutely real, but the crucial distinction is the object of that

12:08.400 --> 12:08.880
regulation.

12:09.440 --> 12:14.760
The biological alarm is sounding, but the alarm is not a gauge reading, warning, low oxytocin.

12:14.820 --> 12:15.560
Oh, got it.

12:15.680 --> 12:20.000
The alarm is a complex network of stress and pain signals urging you to reunite with your

12:20.000 --> 12:24.460
social environment because, evolutionarily speaking, isolation means danger.

12:24.460 --> 12:31.580
Okay, so if the pop science model were actually true, if a hug simply replenished a depleted oxytocin

12:31.580 --> 12:35.960
tank, then the scientific data should be incredibly straightforward, shouldn't it?

12:36.220 --> 12:38.520
We should see a reliable replenishment effect.

12:38.920 --> 12:42.500
You interact with a loved one, your tank fills up, and the metric rises.

12:42.900 --> 12:48.380
It should be a highly predictable input-output relationship, but the actual empirical literature

12:48.380 --> 12:51.760
is entirely chaotic in comparison to that simple model.

12:51.760 --> 12:58.380
The paper relies heavily on a massive systematic review and meta-analysis conducted by Brankova's

12:58.380 --> 13:01.820
team in 2023 to test this exact expectation.

13:02.140 --> 13:04.380
Let's explain how significant this kind of study is.

13:04.720 --> 13:09.600
A meta-analysis isn't just a single experiment with, like, 20 college students in a psychology

13:09.600 --> 13:09.940
lab.

13:10.040 --> 13:13.500
It is a comprehensive statistical synthesis of the entire field.

13:13.680 --> 13:15.560
Yes, and the scale of this data is huge.

13:15.560 --> 13:21.500
The Brankova team looked at 63 different studies that investigated endogenous oxytocin, meaning

13:21.500 --> 13:25.200
the oxytocin your body produces naturally in the context of human social interaction.

13:25.300 --> 13:26.560
Wow, 63 studies.

13:26.960 --> 13:34.480
Yeah, and out of those, 51 studies, encompassing over 3,700 participants, met the rigorous criteria

13:34.480 --> 13:36.180
to enter the quantitative synthesis.

13:36.820 --> 13:39.900
It is one of the most robust looks at this phenomenon to date.

13:39.920 --> 13:41.460
That's a massive sample size.

13:41.460 --> 13:41.920
It is.

13:42.100 --> 13:45.620
They separated the data into causal designs and correlational designs.

13:46.280 --> 13:51.660
In the causal designs, researchers actively brought people into a lab, manipulated a social

13:51.660 --> 13:57.240
interaction like having them engage in a conversation or share a comforting touch or play a trust

13:57.240 --> 14:01.820
game, and then measured what happened to their hormone levels compared to a baseline or a control

14:01.820 --> 14:02.140
group.

14:02.440 --> 14:08.140
And if the tank theory is correct, virtually every one of those causal interactions should result

14:08.140 --> 14:10.240
in a significant spike in oxytocin.

14:10.660 --> 14:12.000
The mechanism should trigger.

14:12.000 --> 14:12.900
But it didn't.

14:12.980 --> 14:13.440
Wait, what?

14:13.640 --> 14:18.580
The meta-analysis found no significant overall hormonal response in those causal designs.

14:18.940 --> 14:22.300
Whether they were comparing participants' levels before and after the social interaction,

14:22.760 --> 14:26.600
or comparing the social interaction group to a control group that just sat quietly in a room,

14:27.060 --> 14:32.240
the dependable universal oxytocin spike simply did not exist in the aggregate data.

14:32.240 --> 14:38.220
Wait, are you saying that putting people in a room to socialize, bond, or hug did not reliably

14:38.220 --> 14:42.720
produce an increase in their oxytocin levels across these dozens of studies?

14:42.820 --> 14:44.080
That's exactly what I'm saying.

14:44.440 --> 14:50.300
In the causal manipulated designs, the overall effect size was indistinguishable from zero.

14:50.500 --> 14:51.820
That is wild.

14:52.040 --> 14:54.700
Now, they did look at correlational designs as well.

14:55.080 --> 14:57.400
These are studies where researchers don't manipulate anything.

14:57.400 --> 15:02.900
They just observe natural variations in behavior and see if they correlate with variations in

15:02.900 --> 15:04.260
hormone levels over time.

15:04.860 --> 15:08.820
In those correlational designs, they did find a small positive association between

15:08.820 --> 15:11.320
social interaction and oxytocin.

15:11.620 --> 15:13.440
Okay, so there is some signal in the noise.

15:13.560 --> 15:15.060
It's not completely fabricated.

15:15.260 --> 15:15.980
There is a signal.

15:16.120 --> 15:20.900
But the authors of the meta-analysis emphasize that this literature is plagued by massive,

15:21.120 --> 15:22.580
unresolved heterogeneity.

15:23.060 --> 15:25.700
Heterogeneity meaning that the results are all over the place.

15:25.700 --> 15:30.300
Like, one study shows a huge spike, another study doing the exact same thing shows a decrease,

15:30.480 --> 15:33.000
and a third study shows absolutely no change at all.

15:33.340 --> 15:33.860
Precisely.

15:34.580 --> 15:39.040
The results fluctuate wildly depending on the specific experimental paradigm used,

15:39.320 --> 15:43.760
the population being studied, the form of social interaction, and even the biological

15:43.760 --> 15:44.840
sampling procedure.

15:45.300 --> 15:48.620
The data stubbornly refuses to conform to a simple narrative.

15:48.940 --> 15:50.500
So what does this all mean for the listener?

15:50.680 --> 15:55.100
Because on one hand, we are saying oxytocin is undeniably involved in social behavior,

15:55.100 --> 16:00.280
but on the other hand, the largest synthesis of data shows that hugging doesn't reliably

16:00.280 --> 16:00.960
increase it.

16:01.300 --> 16:05.720
Are these researchers arguing that oxytocin is actually irrelevant to our daily social

16:05.720 --> 16:06.120
lives?

16:06.500 --> 16:07.900
Not irrelevant, no.

16:07.980 --> 16:08.140
No.

16:08.400 --> 16:11.640
But vastly more complex than a straightforward hydraulic model.

16:12.140 --> 16:17.580
I mean, when a meta-analysis shows massive heterogeneity, it is strong evidence against the phenomenon

16:17.580 --> 16:18.300
being simple.

16:19.340 --> 16:24.060
As the author notes, if hugging reliably filled an oxytocin tank, we would have discovered a

16:24.060 --> 16:26.600
straightforward physiological mechanism decades ago.

16:26.800 --> 16:32.160
But instead, we see increases in some contexts, decreases in others, and null effects in the rest.

16:32.200 --> 16:32.580
Exactly.

16:32.720 --> 16:34.960
The biological range simply does not fall on command.

16:34.960 --> 16:42.600
Okay, so if oxytocin isn't just predictably rising to reward us for hugging, what on earth is it actually doing?

16:43.240 --> 16:47.840
The paper addresses this in a section about what it calls the bad manners of social chemistry,

16:48.200 --> 16:51.980
and I think this completely upends how most people view the brain.

16:52.340 --> 16:54.940
We really have to start by redefining the word social.

16:55.380 --> 17:01.140
Yes, because in everyday language, we use the word social as a synonym for kindness, warmth,

17:01.140 --> 17:06.760
or, you know, pro-social bonding, we think of a social person as someone friendly.

17:07.060 --> 17:07.320
Right.

17:07.820 --> 17:13.060
But in biology and behavioral ecology, the term social is completely emotionally neutral.

17:13.600 --> 17:17.140
It simply refers to any behavior that occurs between members of the same species.

17:17.360 --> 17:20.340
Which means the spectrum of social behavior is incredibly dark sometimes.

17:20.460 --> 17:20.700
Yeah.

17:21.320 --> 17:24.000
Distrusting a stranger is a profoundly social behavior.

17:24.520 --> 17:26.300
Competition for resources is social.

17:26.740 --> 17:30.120
Jealousy, aggression, territoriality, favoritism, suspicion,

17:30.120 --> 17:31.800
these do not happen in a vacuum.

17:32.060 --> 17:35.220
They are deeply complex calculations about our social environment.

17:35.680 --> 17:39.560
And this is where the early utopian picture of oxytocin truly fractured.

17:40.480 --> 17:43.980
Decades ago, early studies suggested that administering oxytocin to humans

17:43.980 --> 17:49.220
magically increased trust in economic games, enhanced empathy, and promoted generosity.

17:49.740 --> 17:52.720
It looked like a biological panacea for human conflict.

17:52.980 --> 17:57.880
But as the research matured and expanded to include different contexts and different populations,

17:57.880 --> 18:00.380
the data took a very dark turn.

18:00.700 --> 18:01.180
It really did.

18:01.460 --> 18:03.140
And this brings us to the salience hypothesis,

18:03.500 --> 18:08.140
which was heavily popularized by researchers like Shemay Tsuuri and Abu Akel around 2016.

18:08.700 --> 18:13.100
I want to spend some time on this because it completely rewrites the job description of this molecule.

18:13.260 --> 18:13.800
Let's do it.

18:14.120 --> 18:16.340
It argues that oxytocin is not a love button.

18:16.620 --> 18:17.940
It's a volume knob.

18:18.120 --> 18:19.020
Oh, I like that.

18:19.200 --> 18:21.020
Let's explore how that works neurologically.

18:21.020 --> 18:26.200
So if I am in a room, there is a massive amount of sensory data hitting my brain, right?

18:26.520 --> 18:27.320
I hear voices.

18:27.620 --> 18:28.740
I see facial expressions.

18:28.960 --> 18:30.020
I notice body language.

18:30.580 --> 18:34.380
The amygdala, the brain's threat and emotion center, has to decide what matters.

18:34.740 --> 18:35.120
Exactly.

18:35.540 --> 18:40.020
And the salience hypothesis suggests that oxytocin essentially turns up the contrast

18:40.020 --> 18:43.840
on whatever social information is currently most relevant.

18:44.360 --> 18:45.880
It directs your attentional resources.

18:45.880 --> 18:49.900
So if you are interacting with someone you already know and trust and the environment

18:49.900 --> 18:53.800
feels safe, oxytocin amplifies those cues of safety.

18:54.340 --> 18:59.400
The social information says approach and bond, and oxytocin facilitates that bonding.

18:59.620 --> 18:59.900
Right.

19:00.000 --> 19:01.540
But what if the environment is not safe?

19:01.820 --> 19:05.100
What if you're interacting with someone from an out group or someone who has previously

19:05.100 --> 19:06.540
acted aggressively towards you?

19:06.600 --> 19:09.400
That is where the love hormone label becomes dangerous.

19:09.740 --> 19:10.140
Exactly.

19:10.480 --> 19:15.340
If the social cues indicate threat or competition, oxytocin amplifies those cues.

19:15.980 --> 19:20.340
In that context, the social information says protect, defend, or attack.

19:20.900 --> 19:25.360
Under these conditions, oxytocin signaling has been strongly associated with increased

19:25.360 --> 19:30.480
ethnocentrism, heightened suspicion, territoriality, and even preemptive aggression.

19:30.760 --> 19:35.840
So if a mother animal is fiercely defending her offspring from a predator, her oxytocin levels

19:35.840 --> 19:37.400
might be incredibly high.

19:37.580 --> 19:41.700
Not because she is feeling peace and love, but because she is highly attuned to the social

19:41.700 --> 19:42.620
threat to her young.

19:42.900 --> 19:43.080
Yes.

19:43.080 --> 19:47.600
It acts as an amplifier for whatever social strategy is currently adaptive.

19:48.600 --> 19:53.400
The source even points to a critical review published by Lange and colleagues in 2022.

19:53.660 --> 19:54.840
Oh, I remember this part.

19:54.900 --> 19:55.020
Yeah.

19:55.060 --> 19:59.060
They reviewed the breadth of this contradictory literature and posed a radical question.

19:59.060 --> 20:05.940
Does oxytocin even deserve to be classified as a distinctively social neuropeptide at all?

20:06.380 --> 20:11.700
Or is it just a general arousal and attention modulator that happens to frequently operate

20:11.700 --> 20:12.880
in social contexts?

20:12.880 --> 20:18.820
That is a staggering paradigm shift because calling it the love hormone isn't just a cute, innocent

20:18.820 --> 20:20.640
simplification for a magazine cover.

20:20.860 --> 20:25.200
It establishes a fundamentally false biological equation in the public consciousness.

20:25.200 --> 20:30.660
It tells people more oxytocin equals more love, less oxytocin equals less love.

20:30.660 --> 20:35.940
And when that equation fails, as it invariably does, given that the molecule can actively drive

20:35.940 --> 20:41.360
suspicion and territoriality depending on the context, the entire concept of craving, it

20:41.360 --> 20:42.300
collapses entirely.

20:42.460 --> 20:46.880
Because why would an organism inherently crave a molecule that, depending on the immediate

20:46.880 --> 20:50.500
environment, might induce crippling social anxiety or aggressive jealousy?

20:50.600 --> 20:50.940
He wouldn't.

20:50.940 --> 20:51.500
Right.

20:51.600 --> 20:56.160
The idea that we are seeking out the chemical itself is logically bankrupt when the chemical's

20:56.160 --> 20:57.480
effects are that volatile.

20:57.860 --> 21:03.060
But if oxytocin is doing all of these complex, contradictory things, if it's modulating aggression

21:03.060 --> 21:08.540
in one context and bonding in another, how are people so confidently walking around diagnosing

21:08.540 --> 21:10.340
themselves with low oxytocin?

21:10.840 --> 21:12.800
That is the million-dollar question.

21:13.160 --> 21:16.860
This actually leads into what I think is the most frustrating part of the whole phenomenon,

21:17.620 --> 21:18.820
the illusion of measurement.

21:18.820 --> 21:24.880
Because you can actually go online right now, pay a laboratory, and mail them a tube of your

21:24.880 --> 21:28.800
saliva, and they will send you back a report detailing your oxytocin levels.

21:29.040 --> 21:33.240
We need to explain why that is, biologically speaking, highly misleading.

21:33.620 --> 21:37.460
Well, to understand the measurement illusion, we have to look at the literal anatomy of how

21:37.460 --> 21:38.500
this hormone operates.

21:39.480 --> 21:43.420
Oxytocin functions in two entirely distinct biological compartments.

21:43.500 --> 21:44.140
Okay, what are they?

21:44.140 --> 21:49.400
It operates centrally, meaning it is released by neurons directly into the brain's circuitry

21:49.400 --> 21:51.200
to modulate neural activity.

21:51.840 --> 21:57.100
And it operates peripherally, meaning it is released into the systemic bloodstream to act

21:57.100 --> 22:03.620
on the body, for instance, to stimulate uterine contractions during childbirth, or milk letdown

22:03.620 --> 22:04.360
during nursing.

22:04.520 --> 22:08.080
And separating those two compartments is the blood-brain barrier, right?

22:08.080 --> 22:13.740
Which is this highly selective, semiperneable membrane that prevents most molecules in

22:13.740 --> 22:16.500
the blood from simply washing into the brain tissue.

22:16.840 --> 22:17.260
Precisely.

22:17.600 --> 22:22.560
So when a company or a researcher takes a swab of your saliva or draws a vial of blood from

22:22.560 --> 22:25.080
your arm, they are measuring peripheral oxytocin.

22:25.940 --> 22:30.580
They are not placing a microscopic meter inside your amygdala and reading a dial that says social

22:30.580 --> 22:32.020
bonding level 40%.

22:32.020 --> 22:36.140
But the assumption is that whatever is floating around in your bloodstream must be a perfect

22:36.140 --> 22:39.140
mirror of what is happening inside your brain's emotional centers.

22:39.340 --> 22:39.480
Right.

22:39.600 --> 22:43.780
And the paper emphasizes that the relationship between central signaling and peripheral concentration

22:43.780 --> 22:47.660
is incredibly complex and often completely disconnected.

22:48.020 --> 22:52.880
Plus, beyond the central peripheral divide, the actual physical act of measuring the molecule

22:52.880 --> 22:55.000
is steeped in methodological chaos.

22:55.000 --> 23:00.460
The paper details how different laboratories use totally different biochemical assays.

23:01.080 --> 23:02.820
Some use radio-immunoassays.

23:03.360 --> 23:06.660
Others use enzyme-linked immunosorbent assays.

23:06.780 --> 23:07.480
It's a mess.

23:07.760 --> 23:11.500
Let's explain what an assay actually is, because it sounds incredibly definitive.

23:12.160 --> 23:14.500
An assay is essentially a chemical test.

23:15.100 --> 23:20.520
To measure a hormone in blood plasma, scientists often use antibodies proteins designed to latch onto

23:20.520 --> 23:22.840
the specific shape of the oxytocin molecule.

23:22.840 --> 23:27.020
If the antibody latches on, the test flags it and counts it.

23:27.420 --> 23:32.160
But the problem is that blood is full of thousands of different proteins and degraded peptide fragments.

23:32.320 --> 23:34.720
And those antibodies can sometimes cross-react.

23:35.000 --> 23:35.200
Yeah.

23:35.260 --> 23:41.500
They might bind to a molecule that is structurally similar to oxytocin, but is not actually biologically

23:41.500 --> 23:42.620
active oxytocin.

23:42.680 --> 23:44.020
Which skews the whole result.

23:44.100 --> 23:44.480
Exactly.

23:44.760 --> 23:49.440
To prevent this, researchers have to perform an extraction process, chemically treating the

23:49.440 --> 23:51.960
sample to isolate the oxytocin before testing it.

23:51.960 --> 23:57.920
But the paper notes that studies using extracted samples often yield radically different concentration

23:57.920 --> 24:01.060
estimates than studies using unextracted samples.

24:01.540 --> 24:05.180
Furthermore, oxytocin degrades incredibly quickly once it's in the blood.

24:05.700 --> 24:07.520
Its half-life is measured in minutes.

24:07.660 --> 24:08.480
It's very unstable.

24:08.480 --> 24:10.800
And its release isn't a steady stream either.

24:10.960 --> 24:11.600
It's pulsatile.

24:11.820 --> 24:13.100
It comes in rapid bursts.

24:13.600 --> 24:18.040
So depending on the exact minute you draw the blood, the extraction method you use, and

24:18.040 --> 24:22.680
the specific antibody assay you buy, you can get wildly different numbers from the exact

24:22.680 --> 24:23.420
same person.

24:23.640 --> 24:26.300
It is the Wild West of biochemical measurement.

24:26.980 --> 24:28.760
The estimates are incredibly fragile.

24:29.540 --> 24:34.840
Yet popular discourse routinely speaks as though drawing a vial of blood provides a definitive,

24:35.360 --> 24:38.100
objective ledger of a person's capacity for love.

24:38.100 --> 24:41.980
Here's where it gets really interesting to me, because the scientific chaos translates

24:41.980 --> 24:44.220
directly into how we speak to each other every day.

24:44.640 --> 24:47.800
Imagine someone comes home after a terrible, isolating day at work.

24:48.180 --> 24:51.340
They drop their bags, look at their partner, and say, I really need a hug.

24:51.760 --> 24:57.960
Today, society immediately translates that subjective, deeply human emotional plea into a biological

24:57.960 --> 24:58.600
diagnosis.

24:59.100 --> 25:00.600
Ah, your oxytocin is low.

25:00.860 --> 25:03.020
It is an astonishing leap of logic.

25:03.380 --> 25:05.420
First of all, nobody has measured anything.

25:05.420 --> 25:09.600
The person didn't centrifuge a vial of their own blood before asking for a hug.

25:09.720 --> 25:10.680
They just felt sad.

25:11.160 --> 25:16.020
And secondly, even if we could instantly and perfectly measure the oxytocin concentration

25:16.020 --> 25:21.340
inside their brain circuitry in that exact moment, discovering a specific concentration

25:21.340 --> 25:25.220
wouldn't magically prove that the concentration was the cause of the sadness.

25:25.760 --> 25:25.900
Right.

25:26.320 --> 25:30.660
To prove that causal relationship, you would have to demonstrate several things simultaneously.

25:30.860 --> 25:31.280
Like what?

25:31.280 --> 25:36.900
You would have to prove that the specific neurochemical state directly generated this objective feeling

25:36.900 --> 25:37.940
of needing comfort.

25:38.440 --> 25:43.240
You'd have to prove that the nervous system was actively monitoring that specific chemical

25:43.240 --> 25:45.640
variable against a target baseline.

25:46.220 --> 25:51.720
And you have to prove that the behavior of hugging was organized entirely around the goal of correcting

25:51.720 --> 25:53.760
that specific chemical deviation.

25:54.120 --> 25:56.000
But we haven't proven any of that.

25:56.000 --> 26:01.680
Instead, we have taken a deeply complex, unobservable emotional state, and we have just slapped

26:01.680 --> 26:03.200
a biochemical noun on it.

26:03.560 --> 26:07.040
We use the word oxytocin instead of comfort or connection.

26:07.480 --> 26:11.160
And because it sounds scientific, we feel like we understand ourselves better.

26:11.340 --> 26:13.100
But we haven't actually explained anything.

26:13.280 --> 26:18.420
We've just replaced a rich emotional reality with a hollow scientific sounding label.

26:18.800 --> 26:20.680
It provides this illusion of precision.

26:20.680 --> 26:25.960
We do this to feel like we have mastery over emotions that are inherently difficult to manage.

26:26.660 --> 26:30.920
And to truly understand why this fallacy is so seductive, the author suggests we have

26:30.920 --> 26:35.800
to look at oxytocin's famous cousin, a molecule that suffers from the exact same conceptual

26:35.800 --> 26:36.480
distortion.

26:37.080 --> 26:37.520
Dopamine.

26:37.960 --> 26:39.080
Ah, dopamine.

26:39.080 --> 26:42.080
Oh, it is the absolute king of internet neuroslang.

26:42.500 --> 26:44.060
We talk about dopamine constantly.

26:44.260 --> 26:46.740
We say that scrolling on social media gives us dopamine hits.

26:47.160 --> 26:48.680
Video games give us dopamine hits.

26:49.100 --> 26:52.280
Eating a piece of chocolate, checking off a to-do list, getting a text message.

26:52.740 --> 26:56.580
Dopamine has effectively become the universal currency of wanting in our culture.

26:57.060 --> 26:59.440
It has been crowned the molecule of pleasure.

27:00.200 --> 27:04.320
But the author breaks down the logical flaw in this popular understanding beautifully.

27:05.480 --> 27:08.060
Dopamine is a crucial neurotransmitter.

27:08.060 --> 27:08.540
Absolutely.

27:09.080 --> 27:14.760
It participates heavily in the neural machinery of learning, reward prediction error, selecting

27:14.760 --> 27:16.260
actions, and pursuing goals.

27:16.500 --> 27:19.280
It is a vital part of the engine that drives motivation.

27:19.680 --> 27:24.780
But substituting the reward itself with the mechanism that pursues the reward is a completely

27:24.780 --> 27:25.740
circular argument.

27:26.140 --> 27:31.360
I want to walk through this circular logic step by step because it is a trap we all fall into

27:31.360 --> 27:32.380
without realizing it.

27:32.460 --> 27:32.840
Let's do it.

27:32.900 --> 27:34.800
Let's take the example of eating a piece of chocolate.

27:35.000 --> 27:36.440
You eat the chocolate and you enjoy it.

27:36.440 --> 27:38.800
If I ask you, why did you pursue that reward?

27:39.880 --> 27:43.800
The folk science explanation says, because dopamine motivated you.

27:44.020 --> 27:44.260
Okay.

27:44.320 --> 27:45.180
We accept that premise.

27:45.460 --> 27:45.560
Yeah.

27:45.680 --> 27:47.540
Dopamine was involved in the motivation.

27:47.760 --> 27:49.040
Then we ask the next question.

27:49.720 --> 27:53.900
Why did the dopamine system motivate you to eat the chocolate rather than a piece of cardboard?

27:54.560 --> 27:59.400
The biological answer is because the nervous system predicted that the chocolate would be

27:59.400 --> 28:00.020
rewarding.

28:00.780 --> 28:01.040
Okay.

28:01.200 --> 28:03.440
So the final question is, what did you really want?

28:03.780 --> 28:07.300
The pop science circular logic concludes, you wanted dopamine.

28:07.860 --> 28:08.100
Right.

28:08.180 --> 28:12.280
It explains the behavior by entirely erasing the actual reward.

28:12.280 --> 28:17.680
It erases the chocolate, the sensory experience of taste, the caloric value, and the subjective

28:17.680 --> 28:18.120
pleasure.

28:18.500 --> 28:23.320
It replaces the real world object of desire with one single component of the physiological

28:23.320 --> 28:26.200
machinery that made the reward matter in the first place.

28:26.200 --> 28:27.880
It is incredibly reductive.

28:27.880 --> 28:30.560
And we do this with an entire like chemical shopping list.

28:30.920 --> 28:35.320
We've created this whole cultural framework of what the author brilliantly terms folk neuroscience.

28:35.700 --> 28:38.980
We've collectively decided that the brain is basically a pharmacy.

28:39.540 --> 28:40.900
Dopamine equals pleasure.

28:41.300 --> 28:42.600
Oxytocin equals love.

28:42.980 --> 28:44.240
Cortisol equals stress.

28:44.720 --> 28:45.880
Serotonin equals happiness.

28:45.880 --> 28:51.740
We treat the human nervous system as if it is merely a collection of labeled chemical reservoirs.

28:52.240 --> 28:57.180
And worse, we treat our ordinary rich human experiences as if they are nothing more than

28:57.180 --> 28:59.400
the conscious surface of a hidden grocery list.

28:59.780 --> 29:01.440
Like, I need more love.

29:01.720 --> 29:03.800
Let me figure out how to top up my oxytocin.

29:03.980 --> 29:05.280
Or, I'm stressed.

29:05.400 --> 29:07.040
My cortisol tank is overflowing.

29:07.140 --> 29:08.040
I need to drain it.

29:08.420 --> 29:11.580
But real neurobiology is so much less obliging than that.

29:11.580 --> 29:16.800
The brain isn't a bartender mixing you a cocktail of happiness and love out of these four specific

29:16.800 --> 29:17.260
bottles.

29:17.660 --> 29:21.800
These chemicals do radically different things depending on which brain region they're acting

29:21.800 --> 29:25.420
in, what receptors they're binding to, and what the environment looks like.

29:25.660 --> 29:27.860
Well, so this raises an important question, right?

29:27.960 --> 29:33.240
Like, if this mechanistic shopping list model of the brain is so scientifically flawed, why

29:33.240 --> 29:36.680
on earth does this myth survive so stubbornly in the public imagination?

29:37.080 --> 29:39.740
I found the paper's answer to this question incredibly profound.

29:39.740 --> 29:43.380
We suffer from a deep cultural bias for the microscopic.

29:44.180 --> 29:50.580
We have this ingrained, almost religious belief that explanations that descend the scales of

29:50.580 --> 29:54.700
physical size going from the psychological level down to the cellular level down to the

29:54.700 --> 29:59.700
molecular level are inherently deeper or truer explanations.

30:00.340 --> 30:03.840
The author illustrates this fallacy with a brilliant analogy about an airport.

30:04.780 --> 30:09.080
Suppose a woman is standing in the arrivals terminal of an airport, waiting for her husband's

30:09.080 --> 30:09.820
flight to land.

30:10.320 --> 30:14.520
Okay, so if you ask someone to explain the situation, the psychological and relational

30:14.520 --> 30:15.980
description is very straightforward.

30:16.680 --> 30:20.240
She misses her husband, they have been apart, and she is waiting to greet him.

30:20.700 --> 30:20.980
Right.

30:21.260 --> 30:24.560
Now imagine we decide that explanation isn't scientific enough.

30:25.060 --> 30:27.060
We decided to send the scale of size.

30:27.360 --> 30:32.040
We describe the ATP metabolism happening in the muscle cells of her legs as she stands in

30:32.040 --> 30:32.480
the terminal.

30:32.480 --> 30:34.920
Let's quickly define ATP for context.

30:35.560 --> 30:41.520
ATP, or adenosine triphosphate, is the primary energy carrier in all living organisms.

30:42.020 --> 30:47.120
When her leg muscles contract to keep her standing upright, her cells are constantly breaking down

30:47.120 --> 30:49.180
ATP molecules to release energy.

30:49.560 --> 30:53.600
It is an incredibly complex, totally necessary biochemical process.

30:53.840 --> 30:56.880
The description of her ATP metabolism is highly scientific.

30:57.340 --> 30:59.380
It takes us to a physically microscopic scale.

30:59.380 --> 31:03.300
But does that biochemical description explain why she is standing at the airport?

31:03.780 --> 31:04.220
Not at all.

31:04.500 --> 31:09.580
The ATP metabolism has absolutely zero explanatory power for why she's at terminal 3 instead of

31:09.580 --> 31:10.520
sitting in her leaving room.

31:11.120 --> 31:15.140
The ATP would be functioning the exact same way if she were standing in line at a grocery

31:15.140 --> 31:15.520
store.

31:15.860 --> 31:18.240
Biology is not inherently deeper than psychology.

31:18.620 --> 31:21.060
They are just answering entirely different levels of questioning.

31:21.440 --> 31:21.960
Precisely.

31:23.060 --> 31:27.680
Biochemistry tells us extraordinary things about the physical constraints of behavior.

31:27.680 --> 31:33.020
It tells us how attachment is maintained over time, how it integrates with memory, and how

31:33.020 --> 31:34.380
sensory cues are processed.

31:35.240 --> 31:38.580
Psychology characterizes the conscious organization of that desire.

31:39.580 --> 31:42.220
Sociology examines the cultural structures of intimacy.

31:43.040 --> 31:48.160
None of these higher-level fields become obsolete or less true just because molecules exist at

31:48.160 --> 31:48.820
a lower level.

31:48.820 --> 31:53.560
The critical mistake we make is assuming that discovering a lower-level participant, like

31:53.560 --> 31:58.500
a neuropeptide, means we have finally discovered what the higher-level phenomenon really is.

31:58.640 --> 32:00.280
We think we've unmasked the illusion.

32:00.520 --> 32:03.120
We think love really is just oxytocin.

32:03.460 --> 32:07.280
And this is not the first time the scientific community has fallen into this exact trap.

32:07.420 --> 32:08.140
No, it is not.

32:08.460 --> 32:14.100
The source points to a fascinating and honestly somewhat wild cautionary tale from the 1960s involving

32:14.100 --> 32:15.860
a substance called scotophobin.

32:16.380 --> 32:20.920
This historical parallel perfectly demonstrates the danger of confusing an experience with a

32:20.920 --> 32:21.280
molecule.

32:21.720 --> 32:25.600
I really want to dive deep into this scotophobin story because it sounds like pure science fiction,

32:25.720 --> 32:27.620
but it was actual published science.

32:27.840 --> 32:29.400
Oh, it was a huge deal at the time.

32:29.440 --> 32:29.680
Right.

32:29.840 --> 32:34.400
So the paper references a 2006 book by Louis Irwin detailing this whole saga.

32:34.940 --> 32:39.800
In the 1960s, researchers were obsessed with finding the physical basis of memory.

32:39.800 --> 32:42.480
So they set up an experiment with rats.

32:42.800 --> 32:44.200
Rats are naturally nocturnal.

32:44.480 --> 32:46.900
They prefer the dark and avoid bright light.

32:47.200 --> 32:47.480
Makes sense.

32:47.880 --> 32:52.000
The researchers took a group of rats and placed them in a box with a dark compartment and a

32:52.000 --> 32:53.120
brightly lit compartment.

32:53.620 --> 32:58.260
Naturally, the rats went into the dark, but when they did, they were given an electric shock.

32:58.800 --> 33:03.740
Over time, through classical conditioning, these rats learned to suppress their natural instincts.

33:04.240 --> 33:07.520
They became terrified of the dark compartment and stayed in the light.

33:07.520 --> 33:10.160
So this is the standard behavioral conditioning paradigm.

33:10.960 --> 33:13.600
The rats acquired a specific psychological memory.

33:14.500 --> 33:15.360
Fear of the dark.

33:15.700 --> 33:15.940
Right.

33:16.280 --> 33:18.760
But here is where it gets absolutely crazy.

33:19.620 --> 33:24.920
The researchers then sacrificed these trained rats, extracted their brains, and ground up the

33:24.920 --> 33:25.500
neural tissue.

33:26.620 --> 33:30.400
Through chemical fractionation, they isolated a specific peptide.

33:30.800 --> 33:35.560
I think it was a sequence of 15 amino acids that seemed to be highly concentrated in the brains

33:35.560 --> 33:36.460
of the trained rats.

33:36.460 --> 33:41.500
They named this peptide scotophobin, from the Greek words for darkness and fear.

33:42.120 --> 33:42.460
Wow.

33:42.760 --> 33:46.540
They literally believed they had isolated the physical molecule of the memory.

33:46.720 --> 33:47.100
Exactly.

33:47.500 --> 33:52.020
And to prove it, they synthesized scotophobin in the lab and injected it into the brains

33:52.020 --> 33:55.060
of untrained, naive rats, rats that had never been shocked.

33:55.660 --> 34:00.280
And according to their early papers, those untrained rats suddenly started avoiding the dark.

34:00.720 --> 34:05.580
The researchers literally believed that a specific, complex psychological memory, the learned fear of

34:05.580 --> 34:10.620
darkness had been distilled into a single chemical compound that could be pipetted out of a beaker

34:10.620 --> 34:12.660
and relocated from one brain to another.

34:12.660 --> 34:15.660
They believed that the memory was the chemical.

34:15.660 --> 34:18.600
And the scientific community was initially captivated by this.

34:18.600 --> 34:27.240
It promised a future where we could, like, learn a language or acquire a skill simply by taking a pill containing the right memory peptides.

34:27.480 --> 34:28.960
But of course, it completely fell apart.

34:29.200 --> 34:31.420
Other labs couldn't replicate the behavioral transfer.

34:31.860 --> 34:34.960
It turned out the peptide wasn't transferring a specific memory of the dark.

34:34.960 --> 34:42.940
It was likely just causing general physiological stress, making the naive rats freeze up and act abnormally in any testing environment.

34:43.400 --> 34:48.480
Yeah, the scotophobin theory lost all credibility because it made a fundamental philosophical error.

34:48.940 --> 34:53.160
It conflated a physiological mediator with an intentional object.

34:53.360 --> 34:57.280
The parallel to our modern understanding of oxytocin is undeniable, isn't it?

34:57.360 --> 34:58.000
It really is.

34:58.440 --> 35:02.000
Where the scotophobin researchers claimed that a specific memory was a chemical,

35:02.000 --> 35:09.560
the pop science oxytocin narrative claims that a specific emotion love or the desire for human connection is a chemical.

35:09.980 --> 35:11.200
It's the exact same mistake.

35:11.300 --> 35:12.240
Just a different decade.

35:12.480 --> 35:16.560
We are still trying to pipette complex human experiences out of a beaker.

35:16.800 --> 35:21.120
And just as the scotophobin theory fractured under the weight of better evidence,

35:21.580 --> 35:24.460
the simplistic oxytocin model is fracturing today.

35:24.560 --> 35:24.980
Exactly.

35:24.980 --> 35:27.440
But this leaves us with a really profound question.

35:28.140 --> 35:30.400
If we strip away the love hormone myth,

35:30.400 --> 35:35.900
if we take away the simple, comforting idea of a chemical shopping list that we can easily top up,

35:36.580 --> 35:37.560
what are we left with?

35:37.680 --> 35:38.680
Are we just left with magic?

35:39.300 --> 35:44.160
Does the desire for affection stop being a physical, biological reality?

35:44.380 --> 35:44.920
Not at all.

35:45.040 --> 35:50.740
In fact, rejecting the oxytocin craving metaphor doesn't remove human sociality from the realm of biology.

35:50.900 --> 35:56.500
It actually forces us to tell a much better, infinitely more fascinating biological story.

35:56.500 --> 36:02.500
And to tell that true biological story, the source brings in insights from a prominent researcher named C. Sue Carter,

36:02.720 --> 36:04.720
specifically her 2022 work.

36:05.440 --> 36:09.200
Carter argues that to understand human connection, we have to zoom way out.

36:09.660 --> 36:13.420
We have to look past the individual molecules, past the individual person,

36:13.420 --> 36:16.620
and look at the evolutionary reality of mammalian development.

36:16.620 --> 36:20.780
Because mammalian development is profoundly, unavoidably social.

36:21.760 --> 36:26.320
Unlike many reptiles or insects that hatch from an egg fully capable of defending themselves,

36:27.160 --> 36:31.940
infant mammals, and human babies in particular, are born entirely helpless.

36:32.180 --> 36:32.900
They really are.

36:33.000 --> 36:36.100
They cannot regulate their own core body temperature.

36:36.340 --> 36:37.740
They cannot forage for food.

36:37.960 --> 36:39.780
They cannot defend themselves against predators.

36:39.780 --> 36:45.180
A human infant, left completely alone in the natural environment, will simply not survive.

36:45.500 --> 36:47.680
It is a biological impossibility.

36:47.920 --> 36:52.420
Therefore, for an infant mammal, proximity to a caregiver is not an optional pleasure.

36:52.660 --> 36:53.500
It is not a luxury.

36:53.920 --> 36:57.180
It is a strict, non-negotiable survival requirement.

36:57.700 --> 37:02.460
So how does a nervous system evolve to handle that kind of intense survival pressure?

37:02.920 --> 37:05.580
If staying close to mom is the only thing keeping you alive,

37:05.580 --> 37:11.780
the brain doesn't just invent a single oxytocin need, cross its fingers, and hope the infant seeks out a hug.

37:11.960 --> 37:15.940
Right. The author points out that a nervous system under that kind of evolutionary pressure

37:15.940 --> 37:20.520
is going to aggressively recruit every single piece of biological machinery it has available.

37:20.880 --> 37:24.120
It creates a massively distributed, over-determined mechanism.

37:24.740 --> 37:31.380
The developing brain recruits the reward circuitry so that social contact feels inherently pleasurable.

37:31.380 --> 37:35.760
It recruits the stress circuitry so that isolation feels terrifying.

37:36.020 --> 37:43.260
It recruits olfactory and auditory recognition systems so the infant can instantly identify the specific scent and sound of its caregiver.

37:43.760 --> 37:48.320
It utilizes endogenous opioids to soothe pain in the presence of the mother.

37:48.980 --> 37:53.000
It uses dopamine to motivate the search for the caregiver, serotonin to regulate mood,

37:53.440 --> 37:58.840
vasopressin for territorial defense, and oxytocin to modulate the salience of all these social cues.

37:58.840 --> 38:01.700
It is an absolute symphony of biology.

38:02.100 --> 38:06.440
All of these systems interact seamlessly to ensure one critical behavioral outcome.

38:07.000 --> 38:10.580
That caregiver proximity feels safe and motivationally central.

38:11.080 --> 38:14.680
And caregiver absence feels intensely physically aversive.

38:15.340 --> 38:19.880
The brain is throwing everything it has at the problem to keep the infant near the person who keeps it alive.

38:19.980 --> 38:25.180
It refuses to collapse all of that distributed, beautiful complexity into the name of one single molecule

38:25.180 --> 38:27.140
just because it's easy to put on a t-shirt.

38:27.140 --> 38:34.240
This brings us back to a strict logical rule about what philosophers call the intentional object of a desire.

38:34.800 --> 38:39.800
The paper puts it beautifully, and it serves as the ultimate refutation of the pop science myth.

38:40.800 --> 38:43.100
A kiss may alter oxytocin signaling.

38:43.400 --> 38:45.340
That is a measurable biological fact.

38:45.780 --> 38:49.880
But that fact does not mean a kiss is merely an oxytocin delivery technology.

38:49.880 --> 38:53.880
Let's sit with that phrase for a second, an oxytocin delivery technology.

38:54.340 --> 38:59.500
Because when we buy into the pop science myth, that is exactly how we are inadvertently treating each other.

38:59.900 --> 39:06.680
If I believe I am craving oxytocin, then my friends, my partner, and my children are reduced to nothing more than dispensing machines

39:06.680 --> 39:09.000
for the chemicals my brain supposedly wants.

39:09.340 --> 39:11.620
But the direction of the explanation matters immensely.

39:12.240 --> 39:14.300
Other people are not proxies for the molecule.

39:14.300 --> 39:20.280
The molecule is just one small part of the vast, complex machinery through which other people matter to us.

39:20.520 --> 39:25.720
Okay, let's summarize the journey we've been on, because we have covered a tremendous amount of ground today.

39:26.060 --> 39:31.140
We started with the love hormones story, which is, admittedly, a really charming little narrative.

39:32.100 --> 39:38.280
Two people embrace, their brains release a specific chemical, and their loneliness is mathematically replenished.

39:38.280 --> 39:41.620
It's clean, it's simple, and it's highly marketable.

39:42.560 --> 39:47.560
But as the author of this paper states, beauty and simplicity are not scientific methods.

39:48.220 --> 39:56.260
Your desire for affection, your deep, aching craving for human connection, is not a craving for a 9-amino acid peptide.

39:56.440 --> 39:59.800
And rejecting this simple metaphor does not make affection any less physical.

40:00.400 --> 40:05.460
As the paper concludes, discarding the myth actually makes the physical explanation more demanding.

40:05.460 --> 40:10.980
Neuroscience does not get the luxury of explaining the person you love away by replacing them with a chemical noun.

40:11.340 --> 40:11.640
Yes.

40:12.480 --> 40:18.540
When you miss someone, your biology is doing incredibly complex, system-wide work to realize that reunion.

40:18.960 --> 40:24.180
Your heart rate, your attention, your memory recall, your stress physiology, your opioid system,

40:24.600 --> 40:27.020
it is all changing dynamically and constantly.

40:27.560 --> 40:31.780
But what you want, what your entire organism is striving for, is them.

40:31.780 --> 40:36.780
The actual person, the messy, complex, irreplaceable human being.

40:37.060 --> 40:44.960
Biology has the daunting task of explaining how it is physically possible that we are wired to want another person so deeply.

40:45.160 --> 40:48.280
It doesn't get to explain the person away as an illusion of chemistry.

40:48.480 --> 40:50.120
That is such a powerful distinction.

40:50.380 --> 40:53.740
And I want to leave you, the listener, with a final thought to really mull over,

40:53.900 --> 40:57.000
building on everything we've just discussed about the true biology of connection.

40:57.000 --> 41:02.300
If our drive for human contact isn't just a simple chemical gauge waiting to be topped up,

41:02.700 --> 41:07.420
if it's actually this massive distributed survival mechanism that evolved over millions of years,

41:07.820 --> 41:11.560
requiring a constant interplay between our nervous system and a stable social environment,

41:12.060 --> 41:15.020
what does that mean for how we treat loneliness in our modern world?

41:15.280 --> 41:19.140
It's a profound question, especially given how isolated modern society has become.

41:19.140 --> 41:23.780
Because it means we can't just, you know, biohack our way out of this isolation.

41:24.120 --> 41:30.880
You can't just take a supplement or buy a weighted blanket to simulate touch or seek out a quick transactional interaction

41:30.880 --> 41:33.920
and expect your biological levels to be fixed.

41:34.780 --> 41:38.720
It suggests that true, deep social nourishment cannot be cheated.

41:39.420 --> 41:42.980
It requires full, messy, complex human environments.

41:42.980 --> 41:50.160
We have to actually do the hard work of building lives, neighborhoods, and communities that support the whole body ecology of connection,

41:50.360 --> 41:53.700
rather than just chasing the next isolated neurochemical hit.

41:53.760 --> 41:56.880
It demands considerably more of us and of our society,

41:57.000 --> 42:00.500
but it also gives so much more meaning back to the reality of our relationships.

42:00.760 --> 42:01.460
It really does.

42:01.740 --> 42:06.080
So next time you hug that close friend after a long week and you feel that wave of physical relief,

42:06.200 --> 42:07.860
you aren't just getting an oxytocin hit.

42:07.860 --> 42:13.520
You are experiencing the full, magnificent, over-determined symphony of your evolutionary history,

42:14.160 --> 42:18.660
firing across countless neural networks, telling you that you are safe and that you are not alone.

42:19.320 --> 42:23.060
Thank you for joining us on this deep dive into the true science of affection.

42:23.300 --> 42:23.820
Stay curious.

